/-!
# The i32 word entry's fixture (`web/step.c`, `wiz-web/1`)

Every browser page runtime exports its i64 step (`export "wiz_loom_step" loomStep`,
`web/playground-ui.wiz` and friends) and links `web/step.c`, which adds the three-word
`wiz_step` the host calls: `(state : i32, payload_ptr : i32, payload_len : i32) -> i32`.

This module is the smallest app of that shape, with `main` so `wiz run` compiles and
links it in one go. `web/framework/step_test.c` is its C half: it calls `wiz_step` with
32-bit words exactly as `web/framework/driver.js` does, calls the i64 entry as the older
hosts do, and prints the comparison. `web/framework/test.sh` runs it.

The state is a `Nat` here, not the pages' record, for one reason: in the C ABI a `Nat`
is kind `n` (a plain 64-bit number), so the test can read the state back and compare
numbers, while the pages' record state is kind `w` (the Wiz value word itself). The shim
does not care which: it moves one 32-bit word from `wiz_step` into `wiz_loom_step` and
back, and the test's plain numbers exercise that word exactly as a page's heap handle
would (in a wasm64 module every handle is below 4 GiB by construction; on a 64-bit host
the heap is not, so the *payload* is placed at a 32-bit address instead — `step_test.c`).
-/
import IO
import Text

link_c "../step.c"
link_c "step_test.c"

/-- The C half (`step_test.c`): it drives `wiz_step`; `main` only prints its report. -/
extern "step_probe" Step.probe : String

/-- The step under test: the state and the event string the host assembled
(`event "\n" index "\n" payload`). -/
def loomStep (s : Nat) (ev : String) : Nat := Nat.add s (String.length ev)

/-- The app's i64 entry — the one the page runtimes export and `wiz_step` wraps. -/
export "wiz_loom_step" loomStep

def main : IO Unit := IO.println Step.probe
